B. Phase diagram
For the upper particle band of symplectic Hopf insula-
tor model of (6), we numerically compute the symplectic
Hopf invariantχ(13) as function of the mass parame-
termand interaction strengthUnand depict the results
in Fig. 2. As the integrand of (13) is gauge-dependent,
we use an adapted numerical algorithm of Refs. [11, 39],
which fixes the gauge in Fourier space, to computeχ(see
App. B for details). As shown in Fig. 2(a), we find that,
away from the non-interacting MRW-limitUn= 0, the
symplectic Hopf invariantχremains integer-quantized
and stable against moderate interaction strengths where
the most stable regimes are close to the center of the non-
interacting topological windows,|m|/J= 0,2, where the
Hopf topology is robust up toUn/J∼0.45. AtUn= 0
the topological windows reproduce the bare MRW result
(4), with gap closings occurring at|m|= 1,3 marking the
transitions. The phase boundaries shift moderately with
increasingUn, as the isotropic pairing terms in (6) pro-
gressively narrow the BBdG gap. The integer-quantized
topological regions of stable symplectic Hopf invariants
χcoincide with regions where the minimum Bogoliubov
quasiparticle energy gap between the lower and upper
particle band, ∆E BdG
min = min k|E+(k)−E −(k)|, stays
open over the Brillouin zone, as shown in Fig. 2(b).
In Fig. 2(c) and (d), we show one-dimensional slices of the
phase diagram at fixedUn/J= 0.2 and fixedm/J= 2,
respectively. These results corroborate the symplectic
Hopf invariantχas the correct invariant for bosonic BdG
systems.
C. Bulk-boundary correspondence and edge states
Now, we study the topologically protected surface
states of the symplectic Hopf insulator model. Note
that in the Hermitian Hopf insulator, the bulk-boundary
correspondence is delicate: the Hopf invariantχdoes
not protect gapless chiral edge modes as a Chern num-
ber would, but it does guarantee that at least|χ|sur-
face modes appear on any boundary [20, 27]. In our
BBdG case, we open the cubic lattice alongz, keeping
k∥ = (kx,ky) as good quantum numbers, via the inverse
DFT method of Ref. [27], and diagonalize the resulting
bosonic BdG slab para-unitarily (10) to obtain the BBdG
quasiparticle spectrumE j(k∥) and the associated slab
Bogoliubov modes|w j(k∥)⟩= (u j,vj)T . In addition to
