Appendix D: Review of bosonic Bogoliubov-de
Gennes (BdG) Hamiltonians and indefinite inner
product spaces
The Bogoliubov Hamiltonian (C22) is quadratic in
bosonic Nambu operators and its diagonalization must
preserve the bosonic commutation relations. We sum-
marize here the algebraic structure of the underlying in-
definite (Krein) inner product space that the main text
uses (see Refs. [42, 43, 45, 46] for more details).
1. Indefinite inner product and Krein space
The Nambu coefficient spaceC 4 is equipped with the
indefinite inner product⟨ψ|τ z|ψ⟩, with the Krein metric
τz =σ z⊗1 2, which is positive on the particle sector and
negative on the hole sector. A vector is Krein-positive
if⟨ψ|τ z|ψ⟩>0 and Krein-negative if⟨ψ|τ z|ψ⟩<0. The
pair (C4,τz) is a Krein space of signature (2,2) for two
bosonic modes per unit cell, generalizing to signature
(n,n) fornmodes [56, 83].
The Bogoliubov transformation Ψ =Wβthat diag-
onalizes ˆH(B) preserves the bosonic commutation rela-
tions [Ψi,Ψ†
j] =τ ij if and only ifWis paraunitary (8),
W†τzW=Wτ zW† =τ z. The fermionic Bogoliubov
problem instead has a positive-definite Nambu metric
and requires unitarity,W †W=1. The indefiniteτ z is a
direct consequence of bosonic statistics with no fermionic
counterpart.
2. Dynamical matrix and pseudo-Hermiticity
Diagonalizing ˆH(B) (C22) reduces to the eigenvalue
problem of the dynamical matrixD(k) =τ zMk (10).
SinceM k =M †
