We sayµisexact dimensionalif there isδ∈[0,∞] withd µ(x) =dµ(x) =δfor
µ-almost everyx, and in this case we set dim(µ) :=δ. Whenµis exact dimen-
sional, dim(µ) is also equal to the Hausdorff, packing, and several other notions of
dimensions ofµ(see e.g. [24, Section 4], [7, Chapter 10]).
Dynamically defined measures are often exact dimensional because the masses
and radii of dynamical neighborhoods are controlled by additive/subadditive quan-
tities along orbits, so their asymptotic rates are governed by ergodic theorems. We
study exact dimensionality of stationary measures onMunder random dynamics.
