defined modulo 2π, into a continuous phase estimate using one of several available phase-unwrapping algorithms,
including the Least Squares Principle Value (LSPV) method used in this work). 23, 33
Figure 9. Block diagram of the closed-loop simulations used to compare the SHWFS and nlCWFS.
The simulations discussed in this section were performed using 20 DM corrections resolved in time. Figure 9
shows an updated block diagram of the light path. Following the initial propagation path to the FSM described
in§2.1, the beam is then propagated to a deformable mirror (DM) withN= 64 actuators linearly across
the telescope diameter. The DM closes the loop by compensating for measured phase aberrations at each
time-step. The DM applies corrections using a proportional-integral (PI) loop gain controller with parameters
selected to balance stability and responsiveness. The WaveProp DM control law includes a leaky integrator
with leak coefficient set toa= 0.998 to preserve DM stroke; a loop gain proportional coefficient ofb= 0.5
