a quantum and classical system can be fundamentally different in a way that could allow
for a novel test of the quantum nature of a given system.
There are many reasons to suspect such a test is possible. Quantum speedups in
various computing problems indeed suggest that we could tell is someone replaced classical
gates with quantum gates from the novel outcomes that become possible with a quantum
computer [57,60,64]. Unfortunately, in most physical systems of interest, the time evolution
is not under our control at a microscopic level, and therefore we need tests that work in
more generic settings 2.
The Leggett-Garg tests [65] serve as one example of time evolution providing a Bell
