It is tempting to describe this difference as a quantum speed-up. After all, the walker
reasons a given value ofxfaster with a well-chosen Hadamard walk than it would with
an unbiased classical walk. However, there are many classical systems in nature which
diffuses ballistically as well. So a measurement of ballistic diffusion cannot be taken as a
quantum signal. For example, a classical random walk where allN∝tsteps are correlated
can easily move a distancex∝tby moving preferentially in one direction.
The issue with jumping to these kinds of conclusions is that we compare classical and
quantum walks while giving them different rules for evolution. Note that classically, we
defined a random walk via a coin flipping experiment, where at each step we flip a new coin.
However, quantum mechanically, we defined the Hadamard walk to be the walk generated
by using just one qubit, which is correlated at each step. Thus, in fact the Hadamard
walk even fails to be Markovian. The entire reason the quantum walk gets the speedup is
because of correlations built in via reusing the same coin. This immediately tells us that if
we rather defined a classical walk which had correlations allowed as well, we could achieve
the quantum speedup as well. On the flip side, a quantum random walk where a new coin
is used every time step is well known to reproduce the classical behavior [107].
The comparison of random walks is also equivalent to the comparison of the classical
and quantum Cram´ er-Rao bound. While there is a similar comparison of
√
