4.3 Origins in Quantum Field Theory
The original SQU test was based on the structure of correlators of a fieldϕ(⃗ x,t) defined
in Equation (2.10). Having now understood the structure of the correlations at the level
of quantum mechanics, we can now return to quantum field theory to identify the same
structures.
An important difference between the classical and quantum theory can be traced to
the resonant Hamiltonian. Given a set of waves with dispersionω=c sk, one can isolate
the resonant Hamiltonian that corresponds only to the terms that conserve energy [79–83].
