classical Fisher matrices are identical for measurements in a single basis. Moreover, for
multi-parameter measurement, the quantum case is less precise due to the uncertainty
principle.
Defining a Common Basis
The second challenge of comparing quantum and classical algorithms is that in a quantum
system, we must define the time evolution so that it can work in any basis. By contrast, a
classical system has a specific basis. For example, position and momentum are physically
distinct and some quantum advantages maybe simply reflect the ability for the quantum
system to solve the problem in another basis where it is easier. However, the classical
system, if allowed to access that basis, may have a similar performance. Thus, this is a
weaker test than an SQU test in the sense that we do not assume the quantum and classical
description are required to be defined in particular bases.
