The Jarzynski estimator converges to the true free energy dif-
ference in the infinite-sample limit, lim n→∞ ∆F (n) =∆F, but
it is typically dominated by rare trajectories, leading to poor
convergence [19–21]. To improve the convergence of the
Jarzynski estimator, various techniques have been proposed
[22], including escorted trajectories [23–25] and importance
sampling of trajectories [26–29]. The time dependence of the
Hamiltonian offers another route for improvement: because
arXiv:2609.10519v1  [cond-mat.stat-mech]  9 Sep 2026
2
the Jarzynski relation holds forarbitrary H(t)that satisfy the
boundary conditionsH(0) =H A andH(T) =H B, it should be
possible to accelerate the convergence of the Jarzynski esti-
mator by optimally choosing the time-dependent sequence of
intermediate Hamiltonians.
Determining optimal intermediate Hamiltonians for non-
equilibrium problems is challenging because, even for simple
systems, the space of all possible time-dependent Hamiltoni-
ans is extremely large. Previous studies in stochastic thermo-
dynamics [7, 30, 31] that sought to minimize various objective
functions through an appropriately designed time-dependent
Hamiltonian have therefore mostly been restricted to a low-
dimensional subspace of parametrized Hamiltonians. In these
approaches, a few external control parametersλ(t)govern
the time dependence of the Hamiltonian [32–36]. Schmiedl
and Seifert analytically determined minimum-dissipation pro-
tocols for an overdamped colloidal particle in a harmonic trap
[32]. Dissipation is quantified by the mean dissipated work,
W dis
≡⟨W⟩−∆F, the mean work in excess of the free en-
ergy difference∆Fbetween the initial and final states. Both
for a moving trap and for a trap with a time-dependent spring
constant, the minimum-dissipation protocols are discontinu-
ous, i.e., the control parameterλ(t)jumps at the initial and
final times. The existence of discontinuities in minimum-
dissipation protocols was later also demonstrated for Brow-
nian particles in anharmonic potentials [35, 37–39], as well as
for underdamped [40] and active [41, 42] particles, and is now
recognized to be a general feature of such protocols [43].
Optimal protocols have also been studied in the context of
non-equilibrium free energy calculations [38, 44–46]. Geiger
and Dellago [38] numerically determined protocols that min-
imize the mean squared error (MSE) between the true free
energy difference and the Jarzynski estimator,
