C. Minimizing the MSE
Finding minimum-error intermediate Hamiltonians re-
quires minimizing the MSE defined in Equation 3 with re-
spect toH(x,τ). In practice, this minimization is challenging
because the search space is exceedingly large: for the Markov
models introduced above,H(x,τ)comprisesN·mindepen-
dent energies. A symmetry inherent in the Markov dynam-
ics yields a simplification: Equation 8 shows that the “gauge
transformation”H(x,τ)→H ′(x,τ) =H(x,τ) +c(τ)with an
arbitraryfunctionc(τ)leaves the Markov dynamics invari-
ant because the transition probabilities depend only on energy
differences. This transformation also leaves the MSE invari-
ant, becausec(τ)shifts all energy levels by the same amount
and thus does not contribute to the fluctuating work. There-
fore,c(τ)can be chosen arbitrarily (“gauge fixing”) and, for
simplicity, the energy of statex=1 is always set to zero, lead-
ing to an optimization problem withN·(m−1)independent
parameters.
Even with the gauge fixing in place, calculating the full
MSE remains demanding. Because∆F (n) depends onn
independent, random work valuesW i, the MSE is ann-
dimensional integral over the joint distribution of the work
values, which is computationally expensive for realisticn≈
102–103. To further simplify the calculation, we followed
the approach of Geiger and Dellago and considered the limit
n≫1, for which the MSE reads [15, 16, 38]
