The solution shows two distinct regimes. If the final energy of
state 2 is close to its initial value (E f→0), the optimal inter-
mediate energy isλ∗∼E f/2 and thus follows the linear inter-
polation. In contrast, when the system is strongly driven out of
equilibrium (Ef→±∞),λ ∗ saturates at±sinh−1(1)≈±0.88,
which is approached exponentially. Thus, the sequence of in-
termediates becomes distinctly asymmetric in time, consisting
of a finite initial jump and a final jump that asymptotically
grows linearly withE f. As shown numerically in the next
subsection, these features persist when multiple intermediate
Hamiltonians are considered.
Equation 23 shows another interesting feature: when the fi-
nal energyE f changes sign, the optimal intermediate energy
λ∗ does as well. This feature is not restricted to the asymp-
totic limits but holds in general, as a consequence of the gauge
invariance (section II C): applying the transformationc(τ) =
(0,−λ∗,−E f)to the optimal HamiltonianH(1,τ) = (0,0,0),
H(2,τ) = (0,λ ∗,E f), we obtainH ′(1,τ) = (0,−λ ∗,−E f),
H′(2,τ) = (0,0,0). After exchanging the labels of states 1 and
2,H′ is the “mirrored” version of the original time-dependent
HamiltonianH:H ′ =−H. This symmetry extends to any
number of intermediates and to arbitrary initial and final en-
ergies; we therefore used it as a consistency check for the nu-
merical results presented in the next subsection.
2. Two-State Model with Multiple Intermediates
For more than a single intermediate, even the simple two-
state model cannot be treated analytically. Therefore, we re-
sorted to the numerical minimization procedure described in
section II C and determined optimal intermediate Hamiltoni-
ansH(2,τ)for different numbers of intermediates,N, dif-
ferent initial energies,E i≡H(2,τ=0), and different final
energies,E f≡H(2,τ=N+1). Specifically, we computed
the optimal intermediate Hamiltonians for all combinations of
N∈{10,20,50,100},E i∈{−8,−6,−4,−2,0,2,4,6,8}, and
Ef∈{−8,−6,−4,−2,0,2,4,6,8}. As a consistency check,
7
we verified that for every combination(N,E i,E f), the mir-
rored intermediates−H(2,τ)agreed within 10 −3 with those
computed for(N,−E i,−E f). All 324 numerically determined
curves are provided in the Supplemental Material (Figure S1
and Figure S2). In the following, we focus on a representative
subset of these results to discuss the trends that hold across
the whole data set.
We first consider the influence of the change in energy,
∆E=E f−E i, on optimal intermediate Hamiltonians for a
fixed number of intermediatesN. Figure 2(a) shows the op-
timal time dependence ofH(2,τ)forN=10,E i =−8 and
different values ofE f. For∆E=0,H(2,τ)is constant. For
∆E=2—a relatively small change in energy—the optimal in-
termediate energies increase approximately linearly withτ.
As∆Eincreases further, the functionH(2,τ)develops jumps
at the initial and final times, with an approximately linear in-
crease in between. The initial jump develops first and sat-
urates atH(2,τ=1)−H(2,τ=0)≈2.6 for∆E≳8. The
final jump, fromτ=10 toτ=11, continues to grow with
∆E, whereas the remaining intermediate energies become in-
dependent of∆E. This behavior is qualitatively similar to
the result obtained for the analytical toy model in the pre-
vious section, where the initial jumpH(2,1)−H(2,0)satu-
rates, whereas the final jumpH(2,2)−H(2,1)grows without
bound. Moreover, the jumps in the optimalH(2,τ)resemble
those of work- and error-minimizing protocols previously ob-
tained for colloidal particles in various potential landscapes
[32, 37, 38]. For work-minimizing protocols, jumps arise
at the boundaries because they enable slower driving in be-
tween, which reduces dissipation. In contrast, for the error-
minimizing protocols, the reason for the jumps is less obvious
and will be discussed below.
Next, we investigated how the optimal intermediate Hamil-
tonians depend on the rate of switching, which is set by the
numberNof intermediates. Figure 2(b) compares the opti-
mal intermediatesH(2,τ)forE i =−8,E f =8, and different
values ofN. To facilitate a direct comparison of the different
curves, the MC time was normalized by the total number of
stepsN+1. The figure shows that, for all values ofN, the
optimalH(2,τ)contains jumps at the initial and final times,
the final jump being much more pronounced than the initial
one. Comparing the curves for different values ofN, the mag-
nitudes of both jumps decrease monotonically withN. Again,
this behavior resembles that of optimal protocols for colloidal
particles, whose jumps also shrink with increasing switching
time [32, 37, 38].
Next, we asked whether—and to what extent—the opti-
mal intermediates actually reduce the true MSE, including the
bias neglected by MSE′. As an example, Figure 3(a) shows
the MSE for linear, logarithmic, and optimal intermediates
as a function of the numbernof trajectories per replica for
Ei =−6,E f =2, andN=10, calculated using MC simulations
(section II D). For all cases, the MSE decays with increasing
n, approaching the power law MSE∝1/nexpected forn→∞.
Three regimes can be distinguished. Forn≲10, the MSE ob-
tained for linear and logarithmic intermediates is smaller than
that for optimal intermediates; for 10≲n≲30, the MSE for
optimal intermediates crosses that for linear and logarithmic
