The poor performance of the optimal intermediates at small
nstems directly from our use of the proxy MSE ′: for small
8
n, the Jarzynski estimator is strongly biased [15, 16, 38], and
for this system, the bias dominates the MSE but is neglected
by MSE′. Asnincreases, the contribution of the bias to the
MSE becomes less important, and MSE′ becomes a better ap-
proximation, eventually leading to optimal intermediates that
outperform the linear and logarithmic ones.
To compare the performance of linear and optimal inter-
mediates across the whole parameter range(N,E i,E f,n), Fig-
ure 3(b) (see also Figure S3) shows the MSE for optimal inter-
mediates against that for linear intermediates. Consistent with
Figure 3(a), the optimal intermediates yield less accurate free
energy estimates than the linear ones for smalln, with an MSE
that is larger by an order of magnitude in extreme cases. For
n≳100, the optimal intermediates consistently outperform
the linear ones. However, the optimal intermediates perform
markedly better only for 10−3 ≲MSE≲5·10 −1 (MSE of the
linear intermediates), whereas for MSE≲10−3, both perform
similarly (Figure S3(a)). Comparing simulations across dif-
ferent values of∆E(Figure S3(c) and (d)) and for different
numbers of intermediatesN(Figure S3(e) and (f)), the im-
provement from optimal intermediates was largest for large
∆Eand smallN. This observation is expected, as for large
∆E, linear and optimal intermediates differ the most. More-
over, for large∆Eand smallN, the system is strongly driven
out of equilibrium, potentially leaving greater room for im-
provement through optimally designed intermediates. Besides
the linear intermediates, we considered logarithmic intermedi-
ates withα=1 andα=2. Results for these MC simulations
are reported in the Supplemental Material (Figure S4 and Fig-
ure S5) and follow the same trends as observed in the lin-
ear case. Surprisingly, logarithmic intermediates performed
worse than linear ones, althoughα=2 approximates the op-
timal sequence of intermediates for free energy perturbation
[47]. This finding shows that the optimal intermediates for
equilibrium-based free energy calculations do not transfer to
the non-equilibrium case.
Previous works in stochastic thermodynamics conjectured
that protocols with a small amount of dissipated work should
also improve non-equilibrium free energy estimates [32, 35].
However, whereas work- and error-minimizing protocols co-
incide for shifted harmonic traps, Geiger and Dellago numer-
ically showed that error-minimizing optimal protocols do not
necessarily minimize the dissipated work, and may even dissi-
pate more work than a naive linear protocol [38]. To study the
dissipation of work for our two-state system, we calculated the
mean work for optimal, linear, and logarithmic intermediates
using the MC simulations. Figure 3(c) shows a correlation
plot of the mean work for optimal and linear intermediates,
which reveals that the mean work for optimal intermediates is
consistently larger than that for linear intermediates. The ob-
served difference in mean work was particularly pronounced
for large values of∆Eand fast switching (see Figure S6(b))—
exactly those cases in which the optimal intermediates achieve
the largest improvement over the linear ones. For logarithmic
intermediates withα=1, we observed the same qualitative
behavior as in the linear case (Figure S7). Logarithmic in-
termediates withα=2 also dissipated more work than opti-
mal intermediates over large ranges of the studied parameter
space, but slightly less for slow switching (Figure S8). Taken
together, these findings emphasize that there is no simple cor-
respondence between work- and error-minimizing intermedi-
ates, and that therefore the dissipated work is not necessarily a
good proxy for the mean error of non-equilibrium free energy
calculations.
B. Double-Well Potential
As a second example, we investigated a three-state system
serving as a toy model of a double-well potential (see Fig-
ure 4(a)). Atτ=0, statex=1 is the global energy min-
imum (barrier height∆ 1) and statex=3 a local minimum
(barrier height∆ 2); atτ=N+1, the two minima have ex-
changed roles. The middle state,x=2, acts as a transi-
tion state. This setup mimics barrier-crossing problems en-
countered, for example, when determining the unfolding free
energy of a nucleic acid molecule in an optical trap [35].
We determined the optimal intermediates for all combina-
tions ofN∈{10,20,50,100},∆ 1∈{2,3,4,5,6}, and∆ 2∈
{1,2,3,4,5}with∆ 2 <∆ 1, amounting to 60 cases. Again, all
numerically determined optimal intermediates are provided in
the Supplemental Material (Figures S9 to S11).
Figure 4(b) shows the optimal intermediates for a represen-
tative example (∆ 1 =6,∆ 2 =3, andN=100). Compared
with the two-state system, the optimal intermediate energies
have a more complex time dependence. Specifically, the en-
ergyH(2,τ)of the transition state (state 2) initially drops
sharply and then continues to decrease, eventually falling be-
low the energy of state 1. Atτ≈90, it begins to increase again
and eventually jumps to its final value. For all 60 parameter
combinations,H(2,τ)varies non-monotonically (Figure S9–
Figure S11); this feature likely accelerates the transition from
state 1 to state 3. The energy of state 3 also varies non-
monotonically and shows small jumps at the initial and final
times. Notably, the energies of states 2 and 3 cross twice dur-
ing the time evolution.
Next, we performed MC simulations to compare the per-
formance of linear, logarithmic, and optimal intermediates for
the double-well potential. Figure 4(c) shows a correlation
plot of the MSE obtained for linear and optimal intermedi-
ates; corresponding plots for the logarithmic intermediates are
provided in the Supplemental Material (Figure S13 and Fig-
ure S14). In contrast to the two-state system, the MSE for
optimal intermediates was lower than that for linear and log-
arithmic intermediates in all cases studied. This difference
suggests that the bias of the Jarzynski estimator is less pro-
nounced for the double-well potential, leading to better per-
formance of optimal intermediates, even for small values ofn.
Moreover, Figure 4(c), Figure S13, and Figure S14 show that,
for the double-well potential, the optimal intermediates fre-
quently reduce the MSE by more than an order of magnitude.
The largest improvement of optimal over linear intermediates
was achieved for large differences in barrier heights,|∆1−∆2|,
that is, for large changes in the energy landscape between the
initial and final Hamiltonians. This finding agrees qualita-
tively with the results for the two-state system. Across differ-
